[31m-# 6. Color matching model[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Why OKLab?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The original website compared colors using Euclidean RGB distance.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The current search converts colors to **OKLab** and computes perceptual distance there. This produces rankings that are more consistent with visible color differences.[m
[31m-[m
[31m----[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Average mode[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For each rendered block + paint image:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. transparent pixels below the alpha threshold are ignored[m
[31m-2. visible sRGB pixels are converted to linear RGB[m
[31m-3. an alpha-weighted mean is computed in linear RGB[m
[31m-4. the result is converted back to sRGB[m
[31m-5. the target and candidate colors are compared in OKLab[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The database also stores the old/simple sRGB mean as `naive_*` values for debugging and comparison.[m
[31m-[m
[31m----[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Dominant mode[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The dominant color is not simply the most frequent exact RGB value.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The current method:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. converts visible pixels to OKLab[m
[31m-2. groups nearby colors with deterministic weighted k-means[m
[31m-3. slightly reduces the influence of the outer image border[m
[31m-4. finds the cluster with the highest weighted mass[m
[31m-5. selects a real source pixel close to that cluster center[m
[31m-6. stores the proportion of visible pixels belonging to the dominant cluster[m
[31m-[m
[31m-This avoids a one-pixel border or tiny RGB variations becoming the "dominant color" by accident.[m
[31m-[m
[31m----[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Dispersion[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Dispersion represents how spread out the visible colors are in OKLab.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-A low value means the texture is relatively uniform.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-A high value means the image contains visibly different colors.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The database stores:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-dispersion_oklab[m
[31m-dispersion_norm[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-`dispersion_norm` is normalized using the 95th percentile of the current dataset so extreme outliers do not make the slider useless.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The last full build used:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-dispersion p95 scale = 0.246059[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m----[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Average-mode score[m
[31m-[m
[31m-In Average mode:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-score =[m
[31m-    OKLab distance[m
[31m-    + dispersion weight[m
[31m-    × normalized dispersion[m
[31m-    × maximum dispersion penalty[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The current maximum additive penalty is:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-0.15 OKLab units[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Therefore:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- `0%` ignores dispersion[m
[31m-- `100%` applies the maximum configured dispersion penalty[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The purpose is to avoid cases where a highly multicolored texture has the correct average color but does not visually resemble the requested flat color.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Dominant mode currently ignores the dispersion penalty.[m
[31m-[m
